---
name: design-stack
description: Entry point for any web UI, frontend, visual design, redesign, polish, animation, motion or UI review 
work in this workspace. Load it before starting such work. It routes to the installed design stack (Impeccable for 
design direction and critique, GSAP skills for motion, Vercel React and web-interface guidelines, Playwright CLI and 
Chrome DevTools for rendered checks, Stitch for exploring directions) and defines the build, look, critique, fix loop.
---

# Design stack

The full reference is `/ai/design-stack/README.md` (from Windows: `\\wsl.localhost\Ubuntu\ai\design-stack\README.md`). 
This skill is the short version.

## Principle

The design decisions are yours whenever Mo asks you for the work. Skills are instruments: they supply vocabulary, 
references and checks. Make every decision deliberately, using skill defaults, templates and style databases as input 
to your own call. `DESIGN.md` and `PRODUCT.md` in the project are the contract; read them before touching UI and keep 
them true.

Governance (`/ai/governance/README.md`) is simple: whichever AI Mo asks owns the task, visual decisions included. On 
shared surfaces, read the project's notes and `KEEP` list and build on them.

## Tool map

| Moment | Use |
|---|---|
| Set up or refresh the contract | `impeccable` â†’ `init` (PRODUCT.md), `document` (DESIGN.md, Google format); lint 
with `npx @google/design.md lint DESIGN.md` |
| Explore directions (new surface or redesign, when asked) | `stitch-design` skills (`generate-design`, 
`code-to-design`, `extract-design-md`) once the Stitch MCP is enabled; otherwise `impeccable shape` / `impeccable 
live` |
| Design vocabulary while building | `impeccable` commands: `craft`, `layout`, `typeset`, `colorize`, `animate`, 
`bolder`, `quieter`, `distill`, `delight`, `overdrive`, `harden`, `adapt` |
| Motion | `gsap-core`, `gsap-timeline`, `gsap-scrolltrigger`, `gsap-plugins`, `gsap-react`, `gsap-performance`, 
`gsap-utils`, `gsap-frameworks` |
| React / Next.js | `vercel-react-best-practices` |
| Look at the result | `playwright-cli` (WSL): `open <url>`, `resize <w> <h>`, `open --device="iPhone 15"`, 
`screenshot --filename=<path>` |
| Deep checks | Chrome DevTools MCP (WSL): `lighthouse_audit`, performance traces, console and network |
| Critique and fix | `impeccable critique`, `impeccable audit` |
| Ship gate | `impeccable polish`, then `web-design-guidelines` |
| Reference lookup (only when explicitly wanted) | `ui-ux-pro-max`: palettes, font pairings, UX rules. Never the style 
source |

Invoke Impeccable as `/impeccable <command>` (Claude and Codex) or `$impeccable <command>` (Codex).

## The loop

1. Read `DESIGN.md` / `PRODUCT.md` (create them with Impeccable if missing and the task is design work).
2. Build in the existing codebase, toward the brief and the bar Mo set.
3. After every visual pass, screenshot at 1440Ã—900 and 390Ã—844 (add 768 when layout changes) and actually inspect 
the images.
4. Run `impeccable critique` and `impeccable audit`; fix what matters; repeat 3â€“4 until nothing material is left.
5. Finish with `impeccable polish`, a `web-design-guidelines` review, a Lighthouse run and a clean console.
6. Update `DESIGN.md` if the visual system changed.

## Environment notes

- WSL (Astra's Pawnther work, Codex CLI, Claude Code in WSL): Playwright CLI uses the bundled Chromium 
(`PLAYWRIGHT_MCP_BROWSER=chromium`; pass `--browser=chromium` if a shell lacks it). Chrome DevTools MCP is registered 
for Codex and Claude. Impeccable hooks run its detector after edits (Codex asks to approve them once under `/hooks`).
- Windows-native Codex app and Claude desktop app: knowledge skills are installed; use the app's own browser for 
screenshots. Impeccable hooks and Playwright CLI live in WSL; the Claude desktop app reaches the Chrome DevTools MCP 
through WSL.
- Stitch MCP is configured but disabled until Mo creates a key.

## Steer

- Build in the project with this stack; it replaces the retired `frontend-design` skill and OpenAI's Sites plugin.
- Grow multi-page sites page by page through the loop, and publish when Mo asks.
- Treat references as inspiration and make the design your own.


